window.LOCKIN = {"version": "2026-11-12a", "module": "EEEN20050 Digital Electronics", "parts": [{"id": "start", "kind": "info", "tab": "path", "title": "Start here", "html": "\n<h3>What the 23/24 paper asked</h3>\n<p>One past paper (Autumn 23/24, Prof. Anding Zhu). 4 questions, equal marks, 120 minutes → <b>30 minutes each</b>. Calculator allowed. No formula sheet is listed, so the flip-flop tables must be in your head.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Q</th><th>What it asked</th><th>Marks</th><th>Topics here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Circuit → Boolean function, truth table + K-map, Σm and ΠM, simplify, two-level NAND</td><td>25%</td><td>1, 2, 3, 4</td></tr><tr><td>2a</td><td>3-bit ripple <b>count-down</b> counter with D flip-flops, waveforms, frequency divider</td><td>15%</td><td>5, 6</td></tr><tr><td>2b</td><td>What causes static hazards, hazard-free circuit for a 4-variable function</td><td>10%</td><td>3, 8</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Synchronous JK state machine with two count loops</td><td>25%</td><td>3, 5, 7</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Asynchronous circuit: transition table, flow table, stability</td><td>25%</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>How often each topic came up (1 paper)</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Share of paper</th><th>Needed by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3 · K-maps</td><td>used in 3 of 4 questions</td><td>Q1, Q2b, Q3</td></tr><tr><td>7 · Synchronous counter design (JK)</td><td>25%</td><td>Q3</td></tr><tr><td>9 · Asynchronous circuits</td><td>25%</td><td>Q4</td></tr><tr><td>6 · Ripple counters</td><td>15%</td><td>Q2a</td></tr><tr><td>8 · Static hazards</td><td>10%</td><td>Q2b</td></tr><tr><td>1 · Circuit → function → truth table</td><td>10%</td><td>Q1a, b</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Two-level NAND</td><td>about 8%</td><td>Q1d</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Σm and ΠM</td><td>5%</td><td>Q1c</td></tr><tr><td>5 · Flip-flops and excitation table</td><td>inside Q2a and Q3</td><td>Q2a, Q3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"warn\"><b>Two warnings.</b> (1) This is one paper, so \"how often\" is thin evidence. (2) You remembered the lecturer saying this year has 5 questions: 1 combinational (maybe multiplexers) and the rest sequential. Multiplexers and sequence detectors were <b>not</b> on the 23/24 paper, so they are in the <b>More practice</b> tab under \"Lecturer hinted\". Do them after the exam path, not instead of it.</div>\n<h3>How to use this page</h3>\n<ol><li>Read the green <b>Learn it</b> box.</li><li>Try the question on paper.</li><li>Reveal the solution <b>one step at a time</b> (button or Space). Each step says why.</li><li>Mark it <b>Got it</b> or <b>Need to redo</b>. Redo the red ones tomorrow.</li><li>Finish with the worked paper, then the two mocks under time.</li></ol>\n<p class=\"keys\">Keys: <kbd>Space</kbd> next step · <kbd>A</kbd> all steps · <kbd>J</kbd>/<kbd>K</kbd> next / previous question · <kbd>G</kbd> got it · <kbd>R</kbd> redo</p>\n"}, {"id": "t1", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "1 · Circuit → Boolean function → truth table", "freq": "Q1(a), (b) · 10%", "learn": "<p>Every gate is a tiny rule. Read the circuit <b>left to right</b>, writing each gate's output on its wire.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/gates.svg\" alt=\"gate symbols\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Output is 1 when…</th><th>Written</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AND</td><td>all inputs are 1</td><td>$AB$</td></tr><tr><td>OR</td><td>any input is 1</td><td>$A+B$</td></tr><tr><td>NOT</td><td>input is 0</td><td>$A'$</td></tr><tr><td>NAND</td><td><b>not</b> all inputs are 1</td><td>$(AB)'$</td></tr><tr><td>NOR</td><td><b>no</b> input is 1</td><td>$(A+B)'$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A <b>bubble</b> (small circle) means \"invert\". De Morgan pushes a bar inside and swaps the operation:</p>\n$$(AB)' = A' + B' \\qquad (A+B)' = A'B'$$\n<p><b>Truth table:</b> n inputs → $2^n$ rows in counting order. Work out F for each row.</p>", "check": {"q": "A NAND gate has inputs 1 and 1. Output?", "a": "<b>0.</b> AND gives 1, the bubble inverts it."}, "items": [{"id": "t1-1", "title": "1.1 Read this circuit", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t1-1.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>(a) Write the Boolean function F. (b) Give its truth table.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The AND gate gives $AB$", "why": "AND: 1 only when all its inputs are 1, written as a product."}, {"do": "The NOR gate gives $(B + C)'$", "why": "NOR = OR, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The last gate is OR. Put the pieces in:$$F = AB + (B + C)'$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}, {"do": "3 inputs → $2^3 = 8$ rows. Write them in counting order 000 … 111.", "why": "Counting order makes row number = minterm number. You need that for part (c)."}, {"do": "Add a helper column for each piece of the function, then combine them:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>$AB$</th><th>$(B + C)'$</th><th>$F$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Small columns stop slips. The final column just applies the last gate to the helper columns."}, {"do": "Tidy with De Morgan if you want (not asked): $F = B'C' + AB$. Same truth table.", "why": "Use it as a check: the simplified form must give the same F column."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(a), (b) style", "answer": "$F = AB + (B + C)'$ · $\\Sigma m(0, 4, 6, 7)$"}, {"id": "t1-2", "title": "1.2 Read this circuit", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t1-2.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>(a) Write the Boolean function F. (b) Give its truth table.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The OR gate gives $A + B'$", "why": "OR: 1 when any input is 1, written as a sum."}, {"do": "The last gate is NAND. Put the pieces in:$$F = ((A + B')C)'$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}, {"do": "3 inputs → $2^3 = 8$ rows. Write them in counting order 000 … 111.", "why": "Counting order makes row number = minterm number. You need that for part (c)."}, {"do": "Add a helper column for each piece of the function, then combine them:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>$F$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Small columns stop slips. The final column just applies the last gate to the helper columns."}, {"do": "Tidy with De Morgan if you want (not asked): $F = C' + A'B$. Same truth table.", "why": "Use it as a check: the simplified form must give the same F column."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(a), (b) style", "answer": "$F = ((A + B')C)'$ · $\\Sigma m(0, 2, 3, 4, 6)$"}, {"id": "t1-3", "title": "1.3 Read this circuit", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t1-3.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>(a) Write the Boolean function F. (b) Give its truth table.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The OR gate gives $A + B$", "why": "OR: 1 when any input is 1, written as a sum."}, {"do": "The NAND gate gives $((A + B)C)'$", "why": "NAND = AND, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The AND gate gives $A'B$", "why": "AND: 1 only when all its inputs are 1, written as a product."}, {"do": "The last gate is OR. Put the pieces in:$$F = ((A + B)C)' + A'B$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}, {"do": "3 inputs → $2^3 = 8$ rows. Write them in counting order 000 … 111.", "why": "Counting order makes row number = minterm number. You need that for part (c)."}, {"do": "Add a helper column for each piece of the function, then combine them:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>$((A + B)C)'$</th><th>$A'B$</th><th>$F$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Small columns stop slips. The final column just applies the last gate to the helper columns."}, {"do": "Tidy with De Morgan if you want (not asked): $F = C' + A'$. Same truth table.", "why": "Use it as a check: the simplified form must give the same F column."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(a), (b) style", "answer": "$F = ((A + B)C)' + A'B$ · $\\Sigma m(0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6)$"}, {"id": "t1-pp", "title": "1.4 Past paper Q1(a)", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Write a Boolean function relating X, Y, Z and F.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The AND gate gives $X'Y'$", "why": "AND: 1 only when all its inputs are 1, written as a product."}, {"do": "The OR gate gives $X + Y$", "why": "OR: 1 when any input is 1, written as a sum."}, {"do": "The NAND gate gives $((X + Y)Z')'$", "why": "NAND = AND, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The last gate is OR. Put the pieces in:$$F = X'Y' + ((X + Y)Z')'$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}, {"do": "Optional tidy-up with De Morgan:$$((X+Y)Z')' = (X+Y)' + Z = X'Y' + Z$$$$F = X'Y' + X'Y' + Z = X'Y' + Z$$", "why": "Not required for part (a), but it makes the truth table quicker and it must match what the K-map gives in part (d)."}], "src": "23/24 Q1(a) · 10%", "answer": "$F = X'Y' + ((X+Y)Z')' = X'Y' + Z$"}]}, {"id": "t2", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "2 · Sum of minterms, product of maxterms", "freq": "Q1(c) · 5%", "learn": "<p>Number the truth-table rows 0, 1, 2 … from the top.</p>\n<p><b>Minterm</b> $m_i$: an AND of all the variables that is 1 <b>only on row i</b>.<br><b>Maxterm</b> $M_i$: an OR of all the variables that is 0 <b>only on row i</b>.</p>\n$$F = \\Sigma m(\\text{rows where } F = 1) \\qquad F = \\Pi M(\\text{rows where } F = 0)$$\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/sigma-pi-switch.svg\" alt=\"switching between the two\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<p>The two lists are opposites: a row number is in one or the other, never both.</p>", "check": {"q": "3 variables, $F = $$\\Sigma m(1, 4, 6)$. Write it as ΠM.", "a": "$\\Pi M(0, 2, 3, 5, 7)$: every row number that is not in the Σ list."}, "items": [{"id": "t2-1", "title": "2.1 Shorthand forms", "prompt": "<p>Express $F = A'B + AC$ as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms (shorthand).</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make the truth table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>F</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Row number in binary = the input values. Evaluate F on each row."}, {"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(A, B, C) = \\Sigma m(2, 3, 5, 7)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(A, B, C) = \\Pi M(0, 1, 4, 6)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(c) style", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(2, 3, 5, 7) = \\Pi M(0, 1, 4, 6)$"}, {"id": "t2-2", "title": "2.2 Shorthand forms", "prompt": "<p>Express $F = (X + Y')(Y + Z)$ as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms (shorthand).</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make the truth table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>X</th><th>Y</th><th>Z</th><th>F</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Row number in binary = the input values. Evaluate F on each row."}, {"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Sigma m(1, 5, 6, 7)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Pi M(0, 2, 3, 4)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(c) style", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(1, 5, 6, 7) = \\Pi M(0, 2, 3, 4)$"}, {"id": "t2-3", "title": "2.3 Shorthand forms", "prompt": "<p>Express $F = AB' + C'D$ as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms (shorthand).</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make the truth table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th><th>F</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Row number in binary = the input values. Evaluate F on each row."}, {"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(A, B, C, D) = \\Sigma m(1, 5, 8, 9, 10, 11, 13)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(A, B, C, D) = \\Pi M(0, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 12, 14, 15)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(c) style", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(1, 5, 8, 9, 10, 11, 13) = \\Pi M(0, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 12, 14, 15)$"}, {"id": "t2-pp", "title": "2.4 Past paper Q1(c)", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Express the function as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms, shorthand format.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "From Q1(b), F = 1 on rows 0, 1, 3, 5, 7.", "why": "The truth table already has everything. Part (c) is just reading it."}, {"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 3, 5, 7)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Pi M(2, 4, 6)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "23/24 Q1(c) · 20%", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(0, 1, 3, 5, 7) = \\Pi M(2, 4, 6)$"}]}, {"id": "t3", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "3 · K-map simplification", "freq": "Used in Q1, Q2(b), Q3", "learn": "<p>A K-map is the truth table folded so that <b>neighbouring cells differ in one variable</b>. That is why the labels go 00, 01, <b>11, 10</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/k3-index.svg\" alt=\"3-variable map\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/k4-index.svg\" alt=\"4-variable map\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<ol><li>Put a 1 in each minterm's cell.</li><li>Circle groups of <b>1, 2, 4 or 8</b>. As big as possible, as few as possible. Edges wrap round.</li><li>For each group keep only the variables that <b>do not change</b>. 0 → primed, 1 → plain.</li><li>OR the groups.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Don't care (x):</b> may be circled if it makes a group bigger, otherwise ignore it.</p>", "check": {"q": "On a 4-variable map, a group of 4 cells gives a term with how many letters?", "a": "<b>2.</b> Each doubling of the group removes one letter: 1 cell = 4 letters, 2 = 3, 4 = 2, 8 = 1."}, "items": [{"id": "t3-1", "title": "3.1 Three variables", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 3, 5, 7)$ with a K-map.</p><p class='note'>Same shape as the past paper.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t3-1-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 1, 3, 5, 7 → $C$</li><li>cells 0, 1 → $A'B'$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = C + A'B'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}], "src": "Used in past paper Q1(d), Q2(b), Q3", "answer": "$F = C + A'B'$"}, {"id": "t3-2", "title": "3.2 Three variables, wrap-around", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C) = \\Sigma m(0, 2, 4, 5, 6)$ with a K-map.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t3-2-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 0, 2, 4, 6 → $C'$</li><li>cells 4, 5 → $AB'$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = C' + AB'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}], "src": "Used in past paper Q1(d), Q2(b), Q3", "answer": "$F = C' + AB'$"}, {"id": "t3-3", "title": "3.3 Four variables", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C, D) = \\Sigma m(0, 2, 5, 7, 8, 10, 13, 15)$ with a K-map.</p><p class='note'>Four corners.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t3-3-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 0, 2, 8, 10 → $B'D'$</li><li>cells 5, 7, 13, 15 → $BD$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = B'D' + BD$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}], "src": "Used in past paper Q1(d), Q2(b), Q3", "answer": "$F = B'D' + BD$"}, {"id": "t3-4", "title": "3.4 Four variables", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C, D) = \\Sigma m(1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 12, 13)$ with a K-map.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t3-4-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 4, 5, 12, 13 → $BC'$</li><li>cells 1, 3, 5, 7 → $A'D$</li><li>cells 4, 5, 6, 7 → $A'B$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = BC' + A'D + A'B$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}], "src": "Used in past paper Q1(d), Q2(b), Q3", "answer": "$F = BC' + A'D + A'B$"}, {"id": "t3-5", "title": "3.5 With don't cares", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C) = \\Sigma m(1)$ with don't cares $d(2, 3, 5, 6, 7)$ with a K-map.</p><p class='note'>This is a real J column from a counter design (Topic 7).</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t3-5-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges. An x can be circled if it helps."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 1, 3, 5, 7 → $C$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = C$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}], "src": "Used in past paper Q1(d), Q2(b), Q3", "answer": "$F = C$"}]}, {"id": "t4", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "4 · Two-level NAND circuits", "freq": "Q1(d) · about 8%", "learn": "<p>Any sum of products becomes NAND-NAND with no extra thought:</p>\n$$F = AB + CD = \\big((AB)' \\cdot (CD)'\\big)'$$\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/nand-transform.svg\" alt=\"AND-OR to NAND-NAND\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<ol><li>Simplify to SOP with the K-map.</li><li>Replace every AND gate with a NAND.</li><li>Replace the OR gate with a NAND.</li><li><b>A term with one letter</b> has no first gate, so <b>invert that letter</b> before the last NAND.</li></ol>\n<p>Why it works: invert twice (no change), then De Morgan turns the OR into \"AND of inverted things, inverted\" = NAND.</p>", "check": {"q": "$F = AB + C$ as NAND-NAND. What goes into the last NAND?", "a": "$(AB)'$ and $C'$. The lone C must be inverted."}, "items": [{"id": "t4-1", "title": "4.1 Two-level NAND", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C) = \\Sigma m(1, 3, 6, 7)$ and implement it with two-level NAND gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t4-1-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = A'C + AB$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Start from the sum of products:$$F = A'C + AB$$", "why": "Two-level NAND always starts from SOP: AND gates into one OR gate."}, {"do": "Invert twice, then use De Morgan on the inner bar:$$F = \\big((A'C)' \\cdot (AB)'\\big)'$$", "why": "Two inversions change nothing. De Morgan turns the OR into an AND of inverted terms, and \"AND then invert\" is exactly a NAND."}, {"do": "Draw it: one NAND per product term, then one NAND that collects them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t4-1-nand.svg\" alt=\"NAND circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Level 1 makes each inverted term. Level 2 NANDs them together."}, {"do": "NOT gates needed for: A.", "why": "The question allows NOT gates for inverted inputs. A NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(d) style", "answer": "$F = A'C + AB$"}, {"id": "t4-2", "title": "4.2 A lone letter Two-level NAND", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C) = \\Sigma m(1, 3, 4, 5, 7)$ and implement it with two-level NAND gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t4-2-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = C + AB'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Start from the sum of products:$$F = C + AB'$$", "why": "Two-level NAND always starts from SOP: AND gates into one OR gate."}, {"do": "Invert twice, then use De Morgan on the inner bar:$$F = \\big(C' \\cdot (AB')'\\big)'$$", "why": "Two inversions change nothing. De Morgan turns the OR into an AND of inverted terms, and \"AND then invert\" is exactly a NAND."}, {"do": "Draw it: one NAND per product term, then one NAND that collects them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t4-2-nand.svg\" alt=\"NAND circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Level 1 makes each inverted term. Level 2 NANDs them together."}, {"do": "Careful with the lone letter $C$: it goes <b>straight into the last NAND as $C'$</b>.", "why": "It has no first-level NAND to invert it, so you invert it yourself. That needs a NOT gate."}, {"do": "NOT gates needed for: B, C.", "why": "The question allows NOT gates for inverted inputs. A NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(d) style", "answer": "$F = C + AB'$"}, {"id": "t4-3", "title": "4.3 Four variables Two-level NAND", "prompt": "<p>Simplify $F(A, B, C, D) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 2, 3, 13, 15)$ and implement it with two-level NAND gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t4-3-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = A'B' + ABD$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Start from the sum of products:$$F = A'B' + ABD$$", "why": "Two-level NAND always starts from SOP: AND gates into one OR gate."}, {"do": "Invert twice, then use De Morgan on the inner bar:$$F = \\big((A'B')' \\cdot (ABD)'\\big)'$$", "why": "Two inversions change nothing. De Morgan turns the OR into an AND of inverted terms, and \"AND then invert\" is exactly a NAND."}, {"do": "Draw it: one NAND per product term, then one NAND that collects them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t4-3-nand.svg\" alt=\"NAND circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Level 1 makes each inverted term. Level 2 NANDs them together."}, {"do": "NOT gates needed for: A, B.", "why": "The question allows NOT gates for inverted inputs. A NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together."}], "src": "Past paper Q1(d) style", "answer": "$F = A'B' + ABD$"}, {"id": "t4-pp", "title": "4.4 Past paper Q1(d)", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Simplify with the K-map and implement with two-level NAND gates. NOT gates allowed.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 1, 3, 5, 7 → $Z$</li><li>cells 0, 1 → $X'Y'$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = Z + X'Y'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Start from the sum of products:$$F = Z + X'Y'$$", "why": "Two-level NAND always starts from SOP: AND gates into one OR gate."}, {"do": "Invert twice, then use De Morgan on the inner bar:$$F = \\big(Z' \\cdot (X'Y')'\\big)'$$", "why": "Two inversions change nothing. De Morgan turns the OR into an AND of inverted terms, and \"AND then invert\" is exactly a NAND."}, {"do": "Draw it: one NAND per product term, then one NAND that collects them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1-nand.svg\" alt=\"NAND circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Level 1 makes each inverted term. Level 2 NANDs them together."}, {"do": "Careful with the lone letter $Z$: it goes <b>straight into the last NAND as $Z'$</b>.", "why": "It has no first-level NAND to invert it, so you invert it yourself. That needs a NOT gate."}, {"do": "NOT gates needed for: X, Y, Z.", "why": "The question allows NOT gates for inverted inputs. A NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together."}], "src": "23/24 Q1(d) · 40%", "answer": "$F = X'Y' + Z = ((X'Y')' \\cdot Z')'$"}]}, {"id": "t5", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "5 · Flip-flops and the excitation table", "freq": "Inside Q2(a) and Q3", "learn": "<p>A <b>flip-flop</b> stores 1 bit (Q). It only changes at the <b>clock edge</b>. Q⁺ means \"Q after the next edge\".</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/seq/ff-symbols.svg\" alt=\"flip-flops\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Flip-flop</th><th>What it does at the edge</th><th>Equation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D</td><td>copies D</td><td>$Q^+ = D$</td></tr><tr><td>T</td><td>T = 1: toggle. T = 0: hold</td><td>$Q^+ = T \\oplus Q$</td></tr><tr><td>JK</td><td>00 hold · 01 reset · 10 set · 11 toggle</td><td>$Q^+ = JQ' + K'Q$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Excitation table</b> = the same facts backwards: \"I want Q to go from this to that. What inputs?\" You need it for every design question.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th><th>D</th><th>T</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "check": {"q": "Q = 1 and you want Q⁺ = 0. What are J and K?", "a": "<b>J = x, K = 1.</b> Reset (01) or toggle (11) both work, so K must be 1 and J can be anything."}, "items": [{"id": "t5-1", "title": "5.1 Follow a JK flip-flop", "prompt": "<p>A JK flip-flop starts at Q = 0. At eight clock edges in a row JK = 10, 00, 11, 01, 11, 11, 00, 10. Give Q after each edge.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Recall the four JK actions: 00 hold, 01 reset, 10 set, 11 toggle.", "why": "J is the set input, K is the reset input. Both on = flip."}, {"do": "Apply one edge at a time, always starting from the previous Q:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Edge</th><th>JK</th><th>Action</th><th>Q after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>set</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>00</td><td>hold</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>11</td><td>toggle</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>01</td><td>reset</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>11</td><td>toggle</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>11</td><td>toggle</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>00</td><td>hold</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>10</td><td>set</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flip-flop only changes on the clock edge, and what it does depends on what it holds now."}], "src": "Skill needed for Q2(a), Q3", "answer": "Q = 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1"}, {"id": "t5-2", "title": "5.2 Build the excitation table", "prompt": "<p>Starting from the JK characteristic table, work out which J K values make Q go 0→0, 0→1, 1→0 and 1→1.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "0 → 0: hold (00) or reset (01) both work. J = 0 in both, K is either → <b>0 x</b>.", "why": "List every action that gives the move, then see which input is the same in all of them."}, {"do": "0 → 1: set (10) or toggle (11). J = 1, K either → <b>1 x</b>.", "why": "Same reasoning."}, {"do": "1 → 0: reset (01) or toggle (11). K = 1, J either → <b>x 1</b>.", "why": "Now Q starts at 1, so K is the input that matters."}, {"do": "1 → 1: hold (00) or set (10). K = 0, J either → <b>x 0</b>.", "why": ""}, {"do": "Shortcut:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th><th>D</th><th>T</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present Q = 0 → J = next Q, K = x. Present Q = 1 → K = opposite of next Q, J = x. D is simply next Q. T is 1 when Q changes."}], "src": "Skill needed for Q3", "answer": "0x, 1x, x1, x0"}, {"id": "t5-3", "title": "5.3 Make a D flip-flop toggle", "prompt": "<p>How do you connect a D flip-flop so that Q flips on every clock edge? Why does that halve the clock frequency?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Connect its own inverted output back to its input:$$D = Q'$$", "why": "A D flip-flop copies D at the clock edge. If D is always the opposite of Q, the next Q is always the opposite of the present Q."}, {"do": "Q flips once per clock edge, so it needs <b>two</b> clock periods to go 0 → 1 → 0.", "why": "One full cycle of Q takes two full cycles of the clock."}, {"do": "So the frequency of Q is half the clock frequency: $f_Q = f / 2$.", "why": "This is the building block of the ripple counter in Q2(a)."}], "src": "Skill needed for Q2(a)", "answer": "$D = Q'$, output at f / 2"}]}, {"id": "t6", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "6 · Ripple counters and frequency division", "freq": "Q2(a) · 15%", "learn": "<p>A <b>ripple counter</b> is a chain of flip-flops that each <b>toggle</b>. Only the first one gets the real clock. <b>Each later one is clocked by the flip-flop before it.</b></p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/seq/ripple4-counter.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>To make it toggle</th><th>Connect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D flip-flop</td><td>$D = Q'$ (its own inverted output)</td></tr><tr><td>T flip-flop</td><td>T = 1</td></tr><tr><td>JK flip-flop</td><td>J = K = 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Flip-flop edge</th><th>Clock next stage from</th><th>Counts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>rising</td><td>Q</td><td><b>down</b></td></tr><tr><td>rising</td><td>Q'</td><td>up</td></tr><tr><td>falling</td><td>Q</td><td>up</td></tr><tr><td>falling</td><td>Q'</td><td><b>down</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Why:</b> counting up, a bit flips when the bit below it <b>falls</b> (carry). Counting down, it flips when the bit below <b>rises</b> (borrow). Pick the clock connection that gives the flip-flop its active edge at that moment.</p>\n<p><b>Frequency divider:</b> every stage halves the frequency. n stages → f ÷ 2ⁿ.</p>", "check": {"q": "Falling-edge flip-flops, each clocked from the previous Q. Up or down?", "a": "<b>Up.</b> A falling edge on Q is a 1 → 0 change, which is when a carry happens."}, "items": [{"id": "t6-1", "title": "6.1 Count up, D flip-flops", "prompt": "<p>Design a 3-bit binary ripple <b>count-up</b> counter using D flip-flops. Show the logic diagram and output waveforms.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make every flip-flop toggle: connect each flip-flop's own $Q'$ back to its D.", "why": "D = Q' means the next value is the opposite of the present one: it toggles on every clock edge it sees."}, {"do": "Clock the first flip-flop from CLK. Clock each later one from the <b>$Q'$ of the one before</b> (rising-edge flip-flops):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t6-1-circuit.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Counting up, a bit must flip when the bit below goes 1 → 0 (a carry). That is a rising edge on Q', so Q' is the clock. With falling-edge flip-flops, swap: use $Q$."}, {"do": "Count sequence: 000 → 001 → 010 → 011 → 100 → 101 → 110 → 111 → 000", "why": "Plain binary counting, wrapping after the last state."}, {"do": "Waveforms:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t6-1-wave.svg\" alt=\"waveforms\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Q₀ flips on every rising clock edge. Each later output flips on the rising edge of its own clock signal. Draw Q₀ first, then build the others from it."}, {"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4},\\quad f_{Q_2} = \\frac{f}{8}$$", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(a) variation", "answer": "D = Q' on each, clock each stage from the previous Q' (rising edge)"}, {"id": "t6-2", "title": "6.2 Count down, T flip-flops, 2 bits", "prompt": "<p>Design a 2-bit ripple count-down counter with T flip-flops. Draw the waveforms.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make every flip-flop toggle: set T = 1.", "why": "T = 1 means toggle on every clock edge it sees."}, {"do": "Clock the first flip-flop from CLK. Clock each later one from the <b>$Q$ of the one before</b> (rising-edge flip-flops):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t6-2-circuit.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Counting down, a bit must flip when the bit below goes 0 → 1 (a borrow). That is a rising edge on Q, so Q is the clock. With falling-edge flip-flops, swap: use $Q'$."}, {"do": "Count sequence: 00 → 11 → 10 → 01 → 00", "why": "The first clock edge from 000 flips every bit when counting down, like 000 − 1 wrapping round."}, {"do": "Waveforms:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t6-2-wave.svg\" alt=\"waveforms\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Q₀ flips on every rising clock edge. Each later output flips on the rising edge of its own clock signal. Draw Q₀ first, then build the others from it."}, {"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4}$$", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(a) variation", "answer": "T = 1, clock stage 2 from Q₀"}, {"id": "t6-3", "title": "6.3 Frequency divider numbers", "prompt": "<p>A 4-stage ripple counter (JK, J = K = 1) is driven by an 800 kHz clock. What frequency is at each output? Which output gives 100 kHz?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4},\\quad f_{Q_2} = \\frac{f}{8},\\quad f_{Q_3} = \\frac{f}{16}$$<p>With f = 800 kHz: $Q_{0}$ = 400 kHz, $Q_{1}$ = 200 kHz, $Q_{2}$ = 100 kHz, $Q_{3}$ = 50 kHz.</p>", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}, {"do": "100 kHz = 800 kHz ÷ 8 = 800 kHz ÷ 2³ → the <b>third</b> stage, Q₂.", "why": "Divide by 2 for each stage you pass through."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(a) last part", "answer": "400, 200, 100, 50 kHz · 100 kHz is at Q₂"}, {"id": "t6-pp", "title": "6.4 Past paper Q2(a)", "prompt": "<p>Design a three-bit binary ripple count-down counter using D flip-flops. Show the logic diagram, draw the output waveforms and briefly explain how this circuit can be used as a frequency divider.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make every flip-flop toggle: connect each flip-flop's own $Q'$ back to its D.", "why": "D = Q' means the next value is the opposite of the present one: it toggles on every clock edge it sees."}, {"do": "Clock the first flip-flop from CLK. Clock each later one from the <b>$Q$ of the one before</b> (rising-edge flip-flops):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2a-circuit.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Counting down, a bit must flip when the bit below goes 0 → 1 (a borrow). That is a rising edge on Q, so Q is the clock. With falling-edge flip-flops, swap: use $Q'$."}, {"do": "Count sequence: 000 → 111 → 110 → 101 → 100 → 011 → 010 → 001 → 000", "why": "The first clock edge from 000 flips every bit when counting down, like 000 − 1 wrapping round."}, {"do": "Waveforms:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2a-wave.svg\" alt=\"waveforms\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Q₀ flips on every rising clock edge. Each later output flips on the rising edge of its own clock signal. Draw Q₀ first, then build the others from it."}, {"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4},\\quad f_{Q_2} = \\frac{f}{8}$$", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}], "src": "23/24 Q2(a) · 60%", "answer": "D = Q' on each flip-flop, each stage clocked by the previous Q, outputs at f/2, f/4, f/8"}]}, {"id": "t7", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "7 · Designing a synchronous counter (JK)", "freq": "Q3 · 25%", "learn": "<p>A <b>synchronous</b> counter: every flip-flop shares the clock. Gates on the J and K inputs decide which bits change. Design = find those gates.</p>\n<ol><li><b>State diagram</b> from the count sequence.</li><li><b>State table:</b> present state → next state, one row per state.</li><li><b>Excitation:</b> for each flip-flop, present bit → next bit gives J K.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div></li><li><b>K-map</b> each J and K (variables = the state bits). x's are free.</li><li><b>Draw</b> the flip-flops and gates.</li><li><b>Unused states</b> are don't cares, then check each one returns to the count.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Two loops (the past paper):</b> nothing new. Put both loops in the same state table. If the loops use all 8 states there are no unused states.</p>", "check": {"q": "Row 011 → 100. What is J K for the middle flip-flop (B)?", "a": "B goes 1 → 0, so <b>J K = x 1</b>."}, "items": [{"id": "t7-1", "title": "7.1 Modulo-5 counter", "prompt": "<p>Design a synchronous counter that counts 000 → 001 → 010 → 011 → 100 → 000 using JK flip-flops. Check the unused states.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-1-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "A counter has no input, so each state has exactly one arrow out."}, {"do": "5 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). Unused: 101, 110, 111 → don't cares.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-1-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = BC \\qquad K_{A} = 1$$$$J_{B} = C \\qquad K_{B} = C$$$$J_{C} = A' \\qquad K_{C} = 1$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-1-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Check the unused states with the final equations:<ul><li>101 → 010 ✓ back in the count</li><li>110 → 010 ✓ back in the count</li><li>111 → 000 ✓ back in the count</li></ul>", "why": "You treated them as don't cares, so the K-maps decided where they go. If power-up or noise lands there, the counter must find its way back."}], "src": "Lecture 17 example · Q3 style", "answer": "$J_{A} = BC$ · $K_{A} = 1$ · $J_{B} = C$ · $K_{B} = C$ · $J_{C} = A'$ · $K_{C} = 1$"}, {"id": "t7-2", "title": "7.2 Modulo-6 counter", "prompt": "<p>Design a synchronous 0 to 5 counter with JK flip-flops. Is it self-correcting?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-2-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "A counter has no input, so each state has exactly one arrow out."}, {"do": "6 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). Unused: 110, 111 → don't cares.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 0 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 1</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-2-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = BC \\qquad K_{A} = C$$$$J_{B} = A'C \\qquad K_{B} = C$$$$J_{C} = 1 \\qquad K_{C} = 1$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-2-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Check the unused states with the final equations:<ul><li>110 → 111 → 000 ✓ back in the count</li><li>111 → 000 ✓ back in the count</li></ul>", "why": "You treated them as don't cares, so the K-maps decided where they go. If power-up or noise lands there, the counter must find its way back."}], "src": "Q3 style", "answer": "$J_{A} = BC$ · $K_{A} = C$ · $J_{B} = A'C$ · $K_{B} = C$ · $J_{C} = 1$ · $K_{C} = 1$"}, {"id": "t7-3", "title": "7.3 Two loops in one circuit", "prompt": "<p>Use JK flip-flops to design a 3-bit synchronous state machine with two modes. Mode 1: 000 → 010 → 011 → 110 → 101 → 000. Mode 2: 001 → 100 → 111 → 001. One circuit only.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-3-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Both loops live in <b>one</b> state table. The circuit has no mode input: whichever loop it starts in, it stays in."}, {"do": "8 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). All 8 codes are used, so <b>no don't-care states</b>.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>1 1 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 1 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 1</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 0</td><td>1 0 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 1</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-3-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = C \\qquad K_{A} = C$$$$J_{B} = C' \\qquad K_{B} = A$$$$J_{C} = B + A \\qquad K_{C} = B' + A'$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t7-3-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Check one row with the equations, for example 000 → 010. ✓", "why": "A quick check catches a K-map slip. The site re-ran all 8 states through these equations."}], "src": "Past paper Q3 variation", "answer": "$J_{A} = C$ · $K_{A} = C$ · $J_{B} = C'$ · $K_{B} = A$ · $J_{C} = B + A$ · $K_{C} = B' + A'$"}, {"id": "t7-pp", "title": "7.4 Past paper Q3", "prompt": "<p>Use JK flip-flops and external gates to design a 3-bit synchronous binary state machine with two modes. Mode 1: 011 → 001 → 010 → 000 → 100 → 011. Mode 2: 111 → 110 → 101 → 111. One circuit block only. Show the design procedure, state table, logic functions and logic diagram. Minimum flip-flops and gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q3-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Both loops live in <b>one</b> state table. The circuit has no mode input: whichever loop it starts in, it stays in."}, {"do": "8 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). All 8 codes are used, so <b>no don't-care states</b>.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 1</td><td>1 1 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 0</td><td>1 0 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 1</td><td>1 1 0</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q3-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = B'C' \\qquad K_{A} = B'C'$$$$J_{B} = C + A \\qquad K_{B} = C' + A'$$$$J_{C} = A \\qquad K_{C} = A'B' + AB$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q3-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Fewest gates: spot the single-gate forms.$$J_{A} = K_{A} = B'C' = (B+C)' \\;\\text{(one NOR)}$$$$K_{B} = A' + C' = (AC)' \\;\\text{(one NAND)}$$$$K_{C} = AB + A'B' = (A \\oplus B)' \\;\\text{(one XNOR)}$$<p>Total: NOR, OR, NAND, XNOR = <b>4 gates</b>. J<sub>C</sub> = A is just a wire.</p>", "why": "The question asks for the minimum number of external gates. De Morgan turns two-term sums of inverted letters into one NAND or NOR, and J<sub>A</sub> and K<sub>A</sub> share the same gate."}, {"do": "Check one row with the equations, for example 011 → 001. ✓", "why": "A quick check catches a K-map slip. The site re-ran all 8 states through these equations."}], "src": "23/24 Q3 · 100%", "answer": "$J_{A} = B'C'$ · $K_{A} = B'C'$ · $J_{B} = C + A$ · $K_{B} = C' + A'$ · $J_{C} = A$ · $K_{C} = A'B' + AB$"}]}, {"id": "t8", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "8 · Static hazards", "freq": "Q2(b) · 10%", "learn": "<p>A <b>hazard</b> is a short glitch at the output. Cause: one input reaches the output along <b>two paths with different delays</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/seq/hazard-glitch.svg\" alt=\"glitch\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Hazard</th><th>Output should</th><th>Actually</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>static-1</td><td>stay 1</td><td>1 → 0 → 1</td></tr><tr><td>static-0</td><td>stay 0</td><td>0 → 1 → 0</td></tr><tr><td>dynamic</td><td>change once</td><td>changes 3 or more times</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>On a K-map:</b> a static-1 hazard exists wherever <b>two neighbouring 1s are in different groups</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/seq/hazard-kmap.svg\" alt=\"bridge\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<p><b>Fix:</b> add one more group that covers both cells (a \"bridge\"). It is redundant in algebra, but it holds the output at 1 during the change.</p>\n<p>Static-0 is the same idea on the 0s of a product-of-sums circuit.</p>", "check": {"q": "$F = XY + X'Z$. Which term removes the hazard?", "a": "<b>$YZ$</b>. Cells 011 and 111 are neighbours in different groups; the pair they form is YZ."}, "items": [{"id": "t8-1", "title": "8.1 Three variables", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F = A'B + AC$ without static-1 hazards, using the fewest gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(2, 3, 5, 7)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-1-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = AC + A'B$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 3 and 7 (only $A$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-1-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $BC$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = AC + A'B + BC$$<p>3 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(b) style", "answer": "$F = AC + A'B + BC$"}, {"id": "t8-2", "title": "8.2 Three variables", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F = XY' + YZ$ without static-1 hazards, using the fewest gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(3, 4, 5, 7)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-2-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = YZ + XY'$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 5 and 7 (only $Y$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-2-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $XZ$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = YZ + XY' + XZ$$<p>3 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(b) style", "answer": "$F = YZ + XY' + XZ$"}, {"id": "t8-3", "title": "8.3 Four variables", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F = B'C' + A'BD + ACD'$ without static-1 hazards, using the fewest gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 14)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-3-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = B'C' + ACD' + A'BD$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 1 and 5 (only $B$ changes)</li><li>cells 8 and 10 (only $C$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-3-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $A'C'D$</p><p>bridge: $AB'D'$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = B'C' + ACD' + A'BD + A'C'D + AB'D'$$<p>5 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(b) style", "answer": "$F = B'C' + ACD' + A'BD + A'C'D + AB'D'$"}, {"id": "t8-4", "title": "8.4 Brackets first", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F = A(B'+CD') + A'D(B+C')$ without static-1 hazards, using the fewest gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Multiply out the brackets:$$F = AB' + ACD' + A'BD + A'C'D$$", "why": "You need plain product terms to fill a K-map."}, {"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(1, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-4-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = B'C'D + ACD' + AB' + A'BD$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 1 and 5 (only $B$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t8-4-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $A'C'D$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = B'C'D + ACD' + AB' + A'BD + A'C'D$$<p>5 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Past paper Q2(b) style", "answer": "$F = B'C'D + ACD' + AB' + A'BD + A'C'D$"}, {"id": "t8-pp", "title": "8.5 Past paper Q2(b)", "prompt": "<p>Briefly explain what causes static hazards. Implement $F(A,B,C,D) = AB'(D'+CD) + A'(B+CD) + A'CD'$ without static-1 hazards, using a minimum number of gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Cause: the same input reaches the output by <b>two paths with different delays</b> (for example one goes through an inverter). When that input changes, one product term turns off slightly before another turns on, and the output glitches for a moment.", "why": "Static-1 hazard: output should stay 1 but dips to 0. Static-0: should stay 0 but pulses to 1. It happens when two neighbouring K-map cells are covered by different groups."}, {"do": "Multiply out the brackets:$$F = AB'D' + AB'CD + A'B + A'CD + A'CD'$$", "why": "You need plain product terms to fill a K-map."}, {"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2b-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = B'C + A'B + AB'D'$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 2 and 6 (only $B$ changes)</li><li>cells 3 and 7 (only $B$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2b-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $A'C$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = B'C + A'B + AB'D' + A'C$$<p>4 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "23/24 Q2(b) · 40%", "answer": "$F = B'C + A'B + AB'D' + A'C$"}]}, {"id": "t9", "kind": "topic", "tab": "path", "title": "9 · Asynchronous circuits: transition table, flow table, stability", "freq": "Q4 · 25%", "learn": "<p>An <b>asynchronous</b> sequential circuit has <b>no clock</b>. It remembers through a <b>feedback wire</b>: an output that loops back in as an input.</p>\n<p>Cut the loop. The same wire now has two names:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Which end</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Y</b> (capital)</td><td>leaving the gate</td><td>next state</td></tr><tr><td><b>y</b> (small)</td><td>coming back in</td><td>present state</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n$$\\text{stable} \\iff Y = y$$\n<p><b>Transition table</b> (lecturer's method):</p>\n<ol><li>Write Y as a function of the inputs and y.</li><li>Map: rows = y, columns = inputs in K-map order (00, 01, 11, 10), entry = Y.</li><li><b>Circle</b> the entries where Y = the row's y.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/seq/transition-table.svg\" alt=\"example\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<p><b>Flow table:</b> the same table with letters (a, b …) for the states, plus the output.</p>\n<p><b>Stability check:</b> look down each <b>column</b>. A column with <b>no circle</b> means the circuit can never settle for that input: it <b>oscillates</b>.</p>", "check": {"q": "$Y = x'y + xy'$. For which x does it oscillate?", "a": "<b>x = 1.</b> Then $Y = y'$: Y is never equal to y. With x = 0, Y = y, always stable."}, "items": [{"id": "t9-1", "title": "9.1 A circuit that remembers", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t9-1.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>(a) Transition table. (b) Flow table. (c) Is there a stability issue?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = AB + Ay + By$$", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t9-1-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 0."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}, {"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span></td><td>b</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span></td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span></td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span></td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable."}, {"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Every column has at least one circle. <b>No stability issue.</b>", "why": "Whatever the inputs, there is a state the circuit can rest in. With one state variable there can be no race either."}], "src": "Past paper Q4 style", "answer": "No stability issue"}, {"id": "t9-2", "title": "9.2 Find the unstable column", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t9-2.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>The output is $z = Ay$.</p><p>(a) Transition table. (b) Flow table. (c) Is there a stability issue?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = A'B + By'$$$$z = Ay$$", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t9-2-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 1."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}, {"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>. Add the output z to each cell.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td>b , 0</td><td>b , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span> , 0</td><td>a , 1</td><td>a , 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable. z is worked out from the inputs and the present row."}, {"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Column AB = <b>11</b> has no circle. In that column y = 0 gives Y = 1 and y = 1 gives Y = 0.", "why": "Each row sends the circuit to the other row, so it never rests."}, {"do": "<b>Yes, there is a stability issue.</b> When AB = 11 the circuit oscillates: y flips 0, 1, 0, 1 … for as long as the inputs stay there. Every other column has a stable state.", "why": "The period is twice the delay round the feedback loop. Say which input combination causes it: that is what the marks are for."}], "src": "Past paper Q4 style", "answer": "Unstable when AB = 11"}, {"id": "t9-3", "title": "9.3 NAND feedback", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t9-3.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>(a) Transition table. (b) Flow table. (c) Is there a stability issue?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = (Ay)'B$$", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/t9-3-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 1."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}, {"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span></td><td>b</td><td>b</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span></td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span></td><td>a</td><td>a</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable."}, {"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Column AB = <b>11</b> has no circle. In that column y = 0 gives Y = 1 and y = 1 gives Y = 0.", "why": "Each row sends the circuit to the other row, so it never rests."}, {"do": "<b>Yes, there is a stability issue.</b> When AB = 11 the circuit oscillates: y flips 0, 1, 0, 1 … for as long as the inputs stay there. Every other column has a stable state.", "why": "The period is twice the delay round the feedback loop. Say which input combination causes it: that is what the marks are for."}], "src": "Lecture 23 slide 18 (renamed inputs)", "answer": "Unstable when AB = 11"}, {"id": "t9-pp", "title": "9.4 Past paper Q4", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q4.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Inputs A and B, one internal state y, output z (the top OR gate: z = A + Y). (a) Derive the transition table and show it on a Karnaugh map. (b) Obtain a flow table. (c) Check whether there is a stability issue and explain where it occurs.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = AB + Ay'$$$$z = A + Y$$<p>(z comes from the top OR gate: inputs A and Y.)</p>", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q4-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 0."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}, {"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>. Add the output z to each cell.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td>b , 1</td><td>b , 1</td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a , 0</td><td>a , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span> , 1</td><td>a , 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable. z is worked out from the inputs and the present row."}, {"do": "Notice z = A + Y = A here.", "why": "Y = A(B + y') can only be 1 when A is 1, so OR-ing it with A adds nothing. The output simply follows A."}, {"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Column AB = <b>10</b> has no circle. In that column y = 0 gives Y = 1 and y = 1 gives Y = 0.", "why": "Each row sends the circuit to the other row, so it never rests."}, {"do": "<b>Yes, there is a stability issue.</b> When AB = 10 the circuit oscillates: y flips 0, 1, 0, 1 … for as long as the inputs stay there. Every other column has a stable state.", "why": "The period is twice the delay round the feedback loop. Say which input combination causes it: that is what the marks are for."}], "src": "23/24 Q4 · 100%", "answer": "Y = AB + Ay' · unstable when AB = 10"}]}, {"id": "pp", "kind": "exam", "tab": "path", "title": "Sample exam · Autumn 23/24 (the real paper)", "freq": "Fully worked", "intro": "<div class=\"rules\"><b>Rules:</b> 120 minutes · answer all 4 · equal marks → about <b>30 minutes per question</b> · non-programmable calculator · no formula sheet. Percentages show the weight of each part inside its question.</div><p>Do it on paper first with a 30-minute timer per question. Then reveal.</p>", "items": [{"id": "pp-1a", "title": "Q1(a) · 10%", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Write a Boolean logic function relating the inputs X, Y, Z and the output F.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The AND gate gives $X'Y'$", "why": "AND: 1 only when all its inputs are 1, written as a product."}, {"do": "The OR gate gives $X + Y$", "why": "OR: 1 when any input is 1, written as a sum."}, {"do": "The NAND gate gives $((X + Y)Z')'$", "why": "NAND = AND, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The last gate is OR. Put the pieces in:$$F = X'Y' + ((X + Y)Z')'$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}, {"do": "Optional tidy-up with De Morgan:$$((X+Y)Z')' = (X+Y)' + Z = X'Y' + Z$$$$F = X'Y' + X'Y' + Z = X'Y' + Z$$", "why": "Not required for part (a), but it makes the truth table quicker and it must match what the K-map gives in part (d)."}], "src": "Topic 1", "answer": "$F = X'Y' + ((X+Y)Z')'$"}, {"id": "pp-1b", "title": "Q1(b) · 30%", "prompt": "<p>Draw the Karnaugh map and the truth table of the logic circuit.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "3 inputs → $2^3 = 8$ rows. Write them in counting order 000 … 111.", "why": "Counting order makes row number = minterm number. You need that for part (c)."}, {"do": "Add a helper column for each piece of the function, then combine them:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>X</th><th>Y</th><th>Z</th><th>$X'Y'$</th><th>$((X + Y)Z')'$</th><th>$F$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Small columns stop slips. The final column just applies the last gate to the helper columns."}, {"do": "Copy each 1 into the K-map cell with the same number:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1-kmap.svg\" alt=\"K-map\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Rows are X, columns are YZ in the order 00, 01, 11, 10 (only one bit changes between neighbours). The small number in each cell is the row number."}], "src": "Topics 1, 3", "answer": "F = 1 on rows 0, 1, 3, 5, 7"}, {"id": "pp-1c", "title": "Q1(c) · 20%", "prompt": "<p>Express the function as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms, shorthand format.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 3, 5, 7)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Pi M(2, 4, 6)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "Topic 2", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(0, 1, 3, 5, 7) = \\Pi M(2, 4, 6)$"}, {"id": "pp-1d", "title": "Q1(d) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Simplify using the Karnaugh map and implement with two-level NAND gates. NOT gates can be used.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 1, 3, 5, 7 → $Z$</li><li>cells 0, 1 → $X'Y'$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = Z + X'Y'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Start from the sum of products:$$F = Z + X'Y'$$", "why": "Two-level NAND always starts from SOP: AND gates into one OR gate."}, {"do": "Invert twice, then use De Morgan on the inner bar:$$F = \\big(Z' \\cdot (X'Y')'\\big)'$$", "why": "Two inversions change nothing. De Morgan turns the OR into an AND of inverted terms, and \"AND then invert\" is exactly a NAND."}, {"do": "Draw it: one NAND per product term, then one NAND that collects them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q1-nand.svg\" alt=\"NAND circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Level 1 makes each inverted term. Level 2 NANDs them together."}, {"do": "Careful with the lone letter $Z$: it goes <b>straight into the last NAND as $Z'$</b>.", "why": "It has no first-level NAND to invert it, so you invert it yourself. That needs a NOT gate."}, {"do": "NOT gates needed for: X, Y, Z.", "why": "The question allows NOT gates for inverted inputs. A NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together."}], "src": "Topics 3, 4", "answer": "$F = X'Y' + Z$"}, {"id": "pp-2a", "title": "Q2(a) · 60%", "prompt": "<p>Design a three-bit binary ripple count-down counter using D flip-flops. Show the logic diagram, draw the output waveforms and briefly explain how this circuit can be used as a frequency divider.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make every flip-flop toggle: connect each flip-flop's own $Q'$ back to its D.", "why": "D = Q' means the next value is the opposite of the present one: it toggles on every clock edge it sees."}, {"do": "Clock the first flip-flop from CLK. Clock each later one from the <b>$Q$ of the one before</b> (rising-edge flip-flops):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2a-circuit.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Counting down, a bit must flip when the bit below goes 0 → 1 (a borrow). That is a rising edge on Q, so Q is the clock. With falling-edge flip-flops, swap: use $Q'$."}, {"do": "Count sequence: 000 → 111 → 110 → 101 → 100 → 011 → 010 → 001 → 000", "why": "The first clock edge from 000 flips every bit when counting down, like 000 − 1 wrapping round."}, {"do": "Waveforms:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2a-wave.svg\" alt=\"waveforms\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Q₀ flips on every rising clock edge. Each later output flips on the rising edge of its own clock signal. Draw Q₀ first, then build the others from it."}, {"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4},\\quad f_{Q_2} = \\frac{f}{8}$$", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}], "src": "Topics 5, 6", "answer": "D = Q', clock from previous Q, f/2, f/4, f/8"}, {"id": "pp-2b", "title": "Q2(b) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Briefly explain what causes static hazards in digital circuits. Implement without static-1 hazards, minimum gates:</p>$$F(A,B,C,D) = AB'(D'+CD) + A'(B+CD) + A'CD'$$", "steps": [{"do": "Cause: the same input reaches the output by <b>two paths with different delays</b> (for example one goes through an inverter). When that input changes, one product term turns off slightly before another turns on, and the output glitches for a moment.", "why": "Static-1 hazard: output should stay 1 but dips to 0. Static-0: should stay 0 but pulses to 1. It happens when two neighbouring K-map cells are covered by different groups."}, {"do": "Multiply out the brackets:$$F = AB'D' + AB'CD + A'B + A'CD + A'CD'$$", "why": "You need plain product terms to fill a K-map."}, {"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2b-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = B'C + A'B + AB'D'$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 2 and 6 (only $B$ changes)</li><li>cells 3 and 7 (only $B$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q2b-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $A'C$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = B'C + A'B + AB'D' + A'C$$<p>4 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Topics 3, 8", "answer": "$F = B'C + A'B + AB'D' + A'C$"}, {"id": "pp-3", "title": "Q3 · 100%", "prompt": "<p>Use JK flip-flops and external gates to design a 3-bit synchronous binary state machine with two modes of operation depending on its initial state.</p><p><b>Mode 1:</b> 011 → 001 → 010 → 000 → 100 → back to 011.<br><b>Mode 2:</b> 111 → 110 → 101 → back to 111.</p><p>No external control. Both modes must use ONE circuit block. Show the design procedure, state table, logic functions and logic diagram. Minimum flip-flops and external gates.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q3-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Both loops live in <b>one</b> state table. The circuit has no mode input: whichever loop it starts in, it stays in."}, {"do": "8 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). All 8 codes are used, so <b>no don't-care states</b>.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 1</td><td>1 1 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 0</td><td>1 0 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 1</td><td>1 1 0</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q3-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = B'C' \\qquad K_{A} = B'C'$$$$J_{B} = C + A \\qquad K_{B} = C' + A'$$$$J_{C} = A \\qquad K_{C} = A'B' + AB$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q3-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Fewest gates: spot the single-gate forms.$$J_{A} = K_{A} = B'C' = (B+C)' \\;\\text{(one NOR)}$$$$K_{B} = A' + C' = (AC)' \\;\\text{(one NAND)}$$$$K_{C} = AB + A'B' = (A \\oplus B)' \\;\\text{(one XNOR)}$$<p>Total: NOR, OR, NAND, XNOR = <b>4 gates</b>. J<sub>C</sub> = A is just a wire.</p>", "why": "The question asks for the minimum number of external gates. De Morgan turns two-term sums of inverted letters into one NAND or NOR, and J<sub>A</sub> and K<sub>A</sub> share the same gate."}, {"do": "Check one row with the equations, for example 011 → 001. ✓", "why": "A quick check catches a K-map slip. The site re-ran all 8 states through these equations."}], "src": "Topics 3, 5, 7", "answer": "$J_{A} = B'C'$ · $K_{A} = B'C'$ · $J_{B} = C + A$ · $K_{B} = C' + A'$ · $J_{C} = A$ · $K_{C} = A'B' + AB$"}, {"id": "pp-4a", "title": "Q4(a) · 40%", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q4.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Two inputs A and B, one internal state y, one output z (top OR gate: z = A + Y). Derive the transition table and show it on a Karnaugh map.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = AB + Ay'$$$$z = A + Y$$<p>(z comes from the top OR gate: inputs A and Y.)</p>", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/pp-q4-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 0."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "$Y = AB + Ay'$"}, {"id": "pp-4b", "title": "Q4(b) · 20%", "prompt": "<p>Obtain a flow table for the circuit.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>. Add the output z to each cell.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td>b , 1</td><td>b , 1</td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a , 0</td><td>a , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span> , 1</td><td>a , 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable. z is worked out from the inputs and the present row."}, {"do": "Notice z = A + Y = A here.", "why": "Y = A(B + y') can only be 1 when A is 1, so OR-ing it with A adds nothing. The output simply follows A."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "see table"}, {"id": "pp-4c", "title": "Q4(c) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Check whether there is a stability issue. If so, explain where the instability can occur.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Column AB = <b>10</b> has no circle. In that column y = 0 gives Y = 1 and y = 1 gives Y = 0.", "why": "Each row sends the circuit to the other row, so it never rests."}, {"do": "<b>Yes, there is a stability issue.</b> When AB = 10 the circuit oscillates: y flips 0, 1, 0, 1 … for as long as the inputs stay there. Every other column has a stable state.", "why": "The period is twice the delay round the feedback loop. Say which input combination causes it: that is what the marks are for."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "Oscillates when AB = 10"}]}, {"id": "mA", "kind": "exam", "tab": "path", "title": "Mock exam A", "freq": "New questions, same style", "intro": "<div class=\"rules\"><b>Rules:</b> 120 minutes · answer all 4 · equal marks → about <b>30 minutes per question</b> · non-programmable calculator · no formula sheet. Percentages show the weight of each part inside its question.</div><p>Unseen questions. Set a 2-hour timer and do the whole paper before revealing anything.</p>", "items": [{"id": "mA-1a", "title": "Q1(a) · 10%", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q1.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Write a Boolean function relating X, Y, Z and F.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The NOR gate gives $(X + Y)'$", "why": "NOR = OR, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The OR gate gives $X' + Z$", "why": "OR: 1 when any input is 1, written as a sum."}, {"do": "The NAND gate gives $((X' + Z)Y)'$", "why": "NAND = AND, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The last gate is OR. Put the pieces in:$$F = (X + Y)' + ((X' + Z)Y)'$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}], "src": "Topic 1", "answer": "$F = (X + Y)' + ((X' + Z)Y)'$"}, {"id": "mA-1b", "title": "Q1(b) · 30%", "prompt": "<p>Draw the Karnaugh map and the truth table.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "3 inputs → $2^3 = 8$ rows. Write them in counting order 000 … 111.", "why": "Counting order makes row number = minterm number. You need that for part (c)."}, {"do": "Add a helper column for each piece of the function, then combine them:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>X</th><th>Y</th><th>Z</th><th>$(X + Y)'$</th><th>$((X' + Z)Y)'$</th><th>$F$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Small columns stop slips. The final column just applies the last gate to the helper columns."}, {"do": "Copy each 1 into the K-map cell with the same number:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q1-kmap.svg\" alt=\"K-map\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Rows are X, columns are YZ in the order 00, 01, 11, 10 (only one bit changes between neighbours). The small number in each cell is the row number."}], "src": "Topics 1, 3", "answer": "F = 1 on rows 0, 1, 4, 5, 6"}, {"id": "mA-1c", "title": "Q1(c) · 20%", "prompt": "<p>Express F as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms (shorthand).</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 4, 5, 6)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Pi M(2, 3, 7)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "Topic 2", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(0, 1, 4, 5, 6) = \\Pi M(2, 3, 7)$"}, {"id": "mA-1d", "title": "Q1(d) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Simplify with the K-map and implement with two-level <b>NAND</b> gates. NOT gates can be used.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q1-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 0, 1, 4, 5 → $Y'$</li><li>cells 4, 6 → $XZ'$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = Y' + XZ'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Start from the sum of products:$$F = Y' + XZ'$$", "why": "Two-level NAND always starts from SOP: AND gates into one OR gate."}, {"do": "Invert twice, then use De Morgan on the inner bar:$$F = \\big(Y \\cdot (XZ')'\\big)'$$", "why": "Two inversions change nothing. De Morgan turns the OR into an AND of inverted terms, and \"AND then invert\" is exactly a NAND."}, {"do": "Draw it: one NAND per product term, then one NAND that collects them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q1-nand.svg\" alt=\"NAND circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Level 1 makes each inverted term. Level 2 NANDs them together."}, {"do": "Careful with the lone letter $Y'$: it goes <b>straight into the last NAND as $Y$</b>.", "why": "It has no first-level NAND to invert it, so you invert it yourself. The inverted form is the plain input, so no gate is needed."}, {"do": "NOT gates needed for: Z.", "why": "The question allows NOT gates for inverted inputs. A NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together."}], "src": "Topics 3, 4", "answer": "$F = Y' + XZ'$"}, {"id": "mA-2a", "title": "Q2(a) · 60%", "prompt": "<p>Design a three-bit binary ripple count-<b>up</b> counter using D flip-flops. Show the logic diagram, draw the output waveforms, and explain how it works as a frequency divider.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make every flip-flop toggle: connect each flip-flop's own $Q'$ back to its D.", "why": "D = Q' means the next value is the opposite of the present one: it toggles on every clock edge it sees."}, {"do": "Clock the first flip-flop from CLK. Clock each later one from the <b>$Q'$ of the one before</b> (rising-edge flip-flops):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q2a-circuit.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Counting up, a bit must flip when the bit below goes 1 → 0 (a carry). That is a rising edge on Q', so Q' is the clock. With falling-edge flip-flops, swap: use $Q$."}, {"do": "Count sequence: 000 → 001 → 010 → 011 → 100 → 101 → 110 → 111 → 000", "why": "Plain binary counting, wrapping after the last state."}, {"do": "Waveforms:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q2a-wave.svg\" alt=\"waveforms\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Q₀ flips on every rising clock edge. Each later output flips on the rising edge of its own clock signal. Draw Q₀ first, then build the others from it."}, {"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4},\\quad f_{Q_2} = \\frac{f}{8}$$", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}], "src": "Topics 5, 6", "answer": "see steps"}, {"id": "mA-2b", "title": "Q2(b) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Explain what causes static hazards. Implement without static-1 hazards, minimum gates:</p>$$F(A,B,C,D) = D(A'B + AC) + B'D'$$", "steps": [{"do": "Cause: one input reaches the output by two paths with different delays. When it changes, one product term switches off before the next switches on, and the output glitches.", "why": "It shows on the K-map as two neighbouring 1s that sit in different groups."}, {"do": "Multiply out the brackets:$$F = A'BD + ACD + B'D'$$", "why": "You need plain product terms to fill a K-map."}, {"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(0, 2, 5, 7, 8, 10, 11, 15)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q2b-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = B'D' + ACD + A'BD$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 7 and 15 (only $A$ changes)</li><li>cells 10 and 11 (only $D$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q2b-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $BCD$</p><p>bridge: $AB'C$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = B'D' + ACD + A'BD + BCD + AB'C$$<p>5 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Topics 3, 8", "answer": "$F = B'D' + ACD + A'BD + BCD + AB'C$"}, {"id": "mA-3", "title": "Q3 · 100%", "prompt": "<p>Use JK flip-flops and external gates to design a 3-bit synchronous state machine with two modes depending on its initial state.<br><b>Mode 1:</b> 010 → 000 → 101 → 011 → 110 → back to 010.<br><b>Mode 2:</b> 111 → 001 → 100 → back to 111.</p><p>One circuit block. Show the state table, logic functions and logic diagram.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q3-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Both loops live in <b>one</b> state table. The circuit has no mode input: whichever loop it starts in, it stays in."}, {"do": "8 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). All 8 codes are used, so <b>no don't-care states</b>.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>1 0 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>1 1 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>1 1 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 1</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 0</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 1</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q3-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = C + B' \\qquad K_{A} = C + B$$$$J_{B} = A \\qquad K_{B} = A'C' + AC$$$$J_{C} = B' \\qquad K_{C} = A'$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q3-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Check one row with the equations, for example 010 → 000. ✓", "why": "A quick check catches a K-map slip. The site re-ran all 8 states through these equations."}], "src": "Topics 3, 5, 7", "answer": "$J_{A} = C + B'$ · $K_{A} = C + B$ · $J_{B} = A$ · $K_{B} = A'C' + AC$ · $J_{C} = B'$ · $K_{C} = A'$"}, {"id": "mA-4a", "title": "Q4(a) · 40%", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q4.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Asynchronous circuit, inputs A and B, internal state y, output $z = Ay'$. Derive the transition table and show it on a Karnaugh map.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = A'B + By$$$$z = Ay'$$", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mA-q4-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 1."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "$Y = A'B + By$"}, {"id": "mA-4b", "title": "Q4(b) · 20%", "prompt": "<p>Obtain a flow table for the circuit.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>. Add the output z to each cell.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td>b , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 1</td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 1</td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span> , 0</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span> , 0</td><td>a , 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable. z is worked out from the inputs and the present row."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "see table"}, {"id": "mA-4c", "title": "Q4(c) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Check whether there is a stability issue. If so, explain where.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Every column has at least one circle. <b>No stability issue.</b>", "why": "Whatever the inputs, there is a state the circuit can rest in. With one state variable there can be no race either."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "No stability issue"}]}, {"id": "mB", "kind": "exam", "tab": "path", "title": "Mock exam B", "freq": "New questions, same style", "intro": "<div class=\"rules\"><b>Rules:</b> 120 minutes · answer all 4 · equal marks → about <b>30 minutes per question</b> · non-programmable calculator · no formula sheet. Percentages show the weight of each part inside its question.</div><p>Unseen questions. Set a 2-hour timer and do the whole paper before revealing anything.</p>", "items": [{"id": "mB-1a", "title": "Q1(a) · 10%", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q1.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 1\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Write a Boolean function relating X, Y, Z and F.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "The NAND gate gives $(XZ)'$", "why": "NAND = AND, then invert. The bubble on the output is the invert."}, {"do": "The AND gate gives $XZ'$", "why": "AND: 1 only when all its inputs are 1, written as a product."}, {"do": "The OR gate gives $Y' + XZ'$", "why": "OR: 1 when any input is 1, written as a sum."}, {"do": "The last gate is AND. Put the pieces in:$$F = (XZ)'(Y' + XZ')$$", "why": "Work from the inputs towards the output. Each gate's output becomes one input of the next gate."}], "src": "Topic 1", "answer": "$F = (XZ)'(Y' + XZ')$"}, {"id": "mB-1b", "title": "Q1(b) · 30%", "prompt": "<p>Draw the Karnaugh map and the truth table.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "3 inputs → $2^3 = 8$ rows. Write them in counting order 000 … 111.", "why": "Counting order makes row number = minterm number. You need that for part (c)."}, {"do": "Add a helper column for each piece of the function, then combine them:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>X</th><th>Y</th><th>Z</th><th>$(XZ)'$</th><th>$Y' + XZ'$</th><th>$F$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Small columns stop slips. The final column just applies the last gate to the helper columns."}, {"do": "Copy each 1 into the K-map cell with the same number:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q1-kmap.svg\" alt=\"K-map\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Rows are X, columns are YZ in the order 00, 01, 11, 10 (only one bit changes between neighbours). The small number in each cell is the row number."}], "src": "Topics 1, 3", "answer": "F = 1 on rows 0, 1, 4, 6"}, {"id": "mB-1c", "title": "Q1(c) · 20%", "prompt": "<p>Express F as a sum of minterms and as a product of maxterms (shorthand).</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Sum of minterms = the rows where F = 1:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 4, 6)$$", "why": "A minterm is 1 on exactly one row. OR together the rows you want to be 1."}, {"do": "Product of maxterms = the rows where F = 0:$$F(X, Y, Z) = \\Pi M(2, 3, 5, 7)$$", "why": "A maxterm is 0 on exactly one row. AND together the rows you want to be 0. The two lists never overlap and together cover every row."}], "src": "Topic 2", "answer": "$\\Sigma m(0, 1, 4, 6) = \\Pi M(2, 3, 5, 7)$"}, {"id": "mB-1d", "title": "Q1(d) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Simplify with the K-map and implement with two-level <b>NOR</b> gates. NOT gates can be used.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Circle the 1s in the biggest groups of 1, 2, 4 or 8:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q1-groups.svg\" alt=\"K-map groups\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Bigger group = fewer letters. Groups may overlap and may wrap round the edges."}, {"do": "Read each group: keep only the letters that stay the same inside it.<ul><li>cells 4, 6 → $XZ'$</li><li>cells 0, 1 → $X'Y'$</li></ul>", "why": "A letter that is 0 in half the group and 1 in the other half cancels out."}, {"do": "OR the groups:$$F = XZ' + X'Y'$$", "why": "Every 1 is now inside at least one group, so this is the simplest sum of products."}, {"do": "Two-level NOR needs product of sums, so group the <b>0s</b>:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q1-zeros.svg\" alt=\"zeros\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Grouping 0s gives the inverse function F'. Inverting that gives POS."}, {"do": "Read the groups as F':$$F' = XZ + X'Y$$", "why": "Same reading rule as for 1s."}, {"do": "Invert with De Morgan:$$F = (X' + Z')(X + Y')$$", "why": "Each product becomes a sum with every letter flipped. The sums are ANDed."}, {"do": "One NOR per sum term, one NOR to collect them:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q1-nor.svg\" alt=\"NOR circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "OR-then-AND turns into NOR-NOR the same way AND-then-OR turns into NAND-NAND."}], "src": "Topics 3, 4", "answer": "$F = (X' + Z')(X + Y')$"}, {"id": "mB-2a", "title": "Q2(a) · 60%", "prompt": "<p>Design a four-bit binary ripple count-<b>down</b> counter using T flip-flops. Show the logic diagram, draw the output waveforms, and explain how it works as a frequency divider. If the clock is 1.6 MHz, what frequency is at each output?</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Make every flip-flop toggle: set T = 1.", "why": "T = 1 means toggle on every clock edge it sees."}, {"do": "Clock the first flip-flop from CLK. Clock each later one from the <b>$Q$ of the one before</b> (rising-edge flip-flops):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q2a-circuit.svg\" alt=\"ripple counter\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Counting down, a bit must flip when the bit below goes 0 → 1 (a borrow). That is a rising edge on Q, so Q is the clock. With falling-edge flip-flops, swap: use $Q'$."}, {"do": "Count sequence: 0000 → 1111 → 1110 → 1101 → 1100 → 1011 → 1010 → 1001 → 1000 → 0111 → 0110 → 0101 → 0100 → 0011 → 0010 → 0001 → 0000", "why": "The first clock edge from 000 flips every bit when counting down, like 000 − 1 wrapping round."}, {"do": "Waveforms:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q2a-wave.svg\" alt=\"waveforms\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Q₀ flips on every rising clock edge. Each later output flips on the rising edge of its own clock signal. Draw Q₀ first, then build the others from it."}, {"do": "Frequency divider: each stage toggles once per full cycle of its clock, so its output has <b>half</b> the frequency.$$f_{Q_0} = \\frac{f}{2},\\quad f_{Q_1} = \\frac{f}{4},\\quad f_{Q_2} = \\frac{f}{8},\\quad f_{Q_3} = \\frac{f}{16}$$<p>With f = 1.6 MHz: $Q_{0}$ = 0.8 MHz, $Q_{1}$ = 0.4 MHz, $Q_{2}$ = 0.2 MHz, $Q_{3}$ = 0.1 MHz.</p>", "why": "n stages divide by 2ⁿ. Take the output from the stage that gives the division you want. It works whether the counter counts up or down."}], "src": "Topics 5, 6", "answer": "see steps"}, {"id": "mB-2b", "title": "Q2(b) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Explain what causes static hazards. Implement without static-1 hazards, minimum gates:</p>$$F(A,B,C,D) = A(B'+CD') + A'D(B+C')$$", "steps": [{"do": "Cause: one input reaches the output by two paths with different delays. When it changes, one product term switches off before the next switches on, and the output glitches.", "why": "It shows on the K-map as two neighbouring 1s that sit in different groups."}, {"do": "Multiply out the brackets:$$F = AB' + ACD' + A'BD + A'C'D$$", "why": "You need plain product terms to fill a K-map."}, {"do": "Fill the K-map:$$F = \\Sigma m(1, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14)$$", "why": "Each product term fills every cell that matches its letters."}, {"do": "Simplify as normal:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q2b-min.svg\" alt=\"minimal\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>$$F = B'C'D + ACD' + AB' + A'BD$$", "why": "This is the cheapest circuit, but it may glitch."}, {"do": "Look for <b>neighbouring 1s that are in different groups</b>:<ul><li>cells 1 and 5 (only $B$ changes)</li></ul>", "why": "Moving between those two cells, one term switches off before the other switches on. For a moment no term is 1, so the output dips to 0. That dip is the static-1 hazard."}, {"do": "Add a group that covers each such pair:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q2b-safe.svg\" alt=\"hazard free\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>bridge: $A'C'D$</p>", "why": "The bridge term stays 1 across the move and holds the output up. Use the biggest group that contains the pair."}, {"do": "Hazard-free answer:$$F = B'C'D + ACD' + AB' + A'BD + A'C'D$$<p>5 AND gates + 1 OR gate (plus NOT gates for inverted inputs).</p>", "why": "It is no longer the minimal SOP. The extra term is redundant in Boolean algebra but needed in real hardware."}], "src": "Topics 3, 8", "answer": "$F = B'C'D + ACD' + AB' + A'BD + A'C'D$"}, {"id": "mB-3", "title": "Q3 · 100%", "prompt": "<p>Use JK flip-flops and external gates to design a 3-bit synchronous state machine with two modes depending on its initial state.<br><b>Mode 1:</b> 000 → 011 → 101 → 110 → back to 000.<br><b>Mode 2:</b> 111 → 100 → 010 → 001 → back to 111.</p><p>One circuit block. Show the state table, logic functions and logic diagram.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the state diagram:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q3-states.svg\" alt=\"states\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Both loops live in <b>one</b> state table. The circuit has no mode input: whichever loop it starts in, it stays in."}, {"do": "8 states → 3 flip-flops ($2^3 = 8$). All 8 codes are used, so <b>no don't-care states</b>.", "why": "Minimum flip-flops = smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of states."}, {"do": "Write the state table. Then for each flip-flop compare its present bit with its next bit and fill J K from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>Q → Q⁺</th><th>J K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 → 0</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 0</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 → 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>next</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>$J_{C}$ $K_{C}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0 0</td><td>0 1 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 0 1</td><td>1 1 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 0</td><td>0 0 1</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>0 1 1</td><td>1 0 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 0</td><td>0 1 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 0 1</td><td>1 1 0</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 0</td><td>0 0 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>1 1 1</td><td>1 0 0</td><td>x 0</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0: only J matters (J = next bit). Present bit 1: only K matters (K = opposite of next bit). The other input is x."}, {"do": "K-map every J and K column (variables A, B, C):<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q3-kmaps.svg\" alt=\"K-maps\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "The x's are free. Circle them whenever that makes a group bigger."}, {"do": "Logic functions:$$J_{A} = C \\qquad K_{A} = C'$$$$J_{B} = 1 \\qquad K_{B} = 1$$$$J_{C} = A' \\qquad K_{C} = A$$", "why": "Each one is read straight off its map."}, {"do": "Logic diagram: three JK flip-flops on one clock, with those functions on the inputs:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q3-circuit.svg\" alt=\"circuit\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Synchronous = every flip-flop shares the clock, so all bits change on the same edge."}, {"do": "Check one row with the equations, for example 000 → 011. ✓", "why": "A quick check catches a K-map slip. The site re-ran all 8 states through these equations."}], "src": "Topics 3, 5, 7", "answer": "$J_{A} = C$ · $K_{A} = C'$ · $J_{B} = 1$ · $K_{B} = 1$ · $J_{C} = A'$ · $K_{C} = A$"}, {"id": "mB-4a", "title": "Q4(a) · 40%", "prompt": "<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q4.svg\" alt=\"Fig. 2\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><p>Asynchronous circuit, inputs A and B, internal state y, output $z = A + y$. Derive the transition table and show it on a Karnaugh map.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Name the two ends of the feedback wire: the gate output is <b>Y</b> (next state), the wire coming back in is <b>y</b> (present state). Read the circuit:$$Y = AB + (A \\oplus B)y'$$$$z = A + y$$", "why": "With the loop cut, the circuit is plain combinational logic with inputs A, B and y."}, {"do": "Make a map: rows y = 0, 1 and columns AB = 00, 01, 11, 10. Fill each cell with Y:<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"fig/mB-q4-tt.svg\" alt=\"transition table\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>", "why": "Columns go in K-map (Gray) order so only one input changes between neighbours. Example cell y = 0, AB = 01: Y = 1."}, {"do": "Circle every cell where Y equals the row's y. Those are the <b>stable</b> states.", "why": "Stable means the next state equals the present state, so nothing is trying to change."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "$Y = AB + (A \\oplus B)y'$"}, {"id": "mB-4b", "title": "Q4(b) · 20%", "prompt": "<p>Obtain a flow table for the circuit.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Rename the states with letters: y = 0 → <b>a</b>, y = 1 → <b>b</b>. Add the output z to each cell.<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt flow\"><thead><tr><th>y \\ AB</th><th>00</th><th>01</th><th>11</th><th>10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"stable\">a</span> , 0</td><td>b , 0</td><td>b , 1</td><td>b , 1</td></tr><tr><td><b>b</b></td><td>a , 1</td><td>a , 1</td><td><span class=\"stable\">b</span> , 1</td><td>a , 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "A flow table is the transition table with letters instead of binary codes. Boxed letter = stable. z is worked out from the inputs and the present row."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "see table"}, {"id": "mB-4c", "title": "Q4(c) · 40%", "prompt": "<p>Check whether there is a stability issue. If so, explain where.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "Check each <b>column</b> for at least one stable (circled) entry.", "why": "For a fixed input the circuit moves up and down inside one column. It can only settle if that column has a circle."}, {"do": "Columns AB = <b>01, 10</b> have no circle. In that column y = 0 gives Y = 1 and y = 1 gives Y = 0.", "why": "Each row sends the circuit to the other row, so it never rests."}, {"do": "<b>Yes, there is a stability issue.</b> When AB = 01, 10 the circuit oscillates: y flips 0, 1, 0, 1 … for as long as the inputs stay there. Every other column has a stable state.", "why": "The period is twice the delay round the feedback loop. Say which input combination causes it: that is what the marks are for."}], "src": "Topic 9", "answer": "Oscillates when AB = 01, 10"}]}, {"id": "more-info", "kind": "info", "tab": "more", "title": "What is here and why", "html": "\n<p>There are <b>no homework sheets</b> for this module, so this tab holds what was left off the exam path.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Left off the exam path</th><th>Why</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Multiplexers</td><td>Not on the 23/24 paper. Lecturer hinted it may be this year's combinational question.</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Sequence detectors (Mealy / Moore)</td><td>Not on the 23/24 paper. Biggest topic in Lectures 14–16, so a likely swap for the counter question.</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Analysing a clocked circuit</td><td>Not on the paper. Short, and it is design backwards.</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Registers, ring and Johnson counters</td><td>Not on the paper.</td><td><a href='https://github.com/albinjoby82-ops/DE_Lock-In/blob/main/notes/17-registers.md'>notes 17</a></td></tr><tr><td>Memory, programmable logic, transistor logic</td><td>Not on the paper. Facts only.</td><td><a href='https://github.com/albinjoby82-ops/DE_Lock-In/blob/main/notes/18-memory.md'>18</a>, <a href='https://github.com/albinjoby82-ops/DE_Lock-In/blob/main/notes/19-programmable-logic.md'>19</a>, <a href='https://github.com/albinjoby82-ops/DE_Lock-In/blob/main/notes/21-transistor-logic.md'>21</a></td></tr><tr><td>Races (critical / non-critical)</td><td>Q4 had one state variable, so no race was possible. Know the definitions.</td><td><a href='https://github.com/albinjoby82-ops/DE_Lock-In/blob/main/notes/20-asynchronous-logic.md'>notes 20 §4</a></td></tr><tr><td>Number systems, Boolean algebra drills, adders, decoders</td><td>Mid-term material. Only K-maps, minterms and NAND carried into the final paper.</td><td><a href='../index.html'>mid-term site</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>More drill on K-maps and NAND: the <a href=\"../index.html\">mid-term quiz site</a> (Topics 7, 8, 9) and the 375 worked <a href=\"../index.html#book-questions\">Mano book questions</a>.</p>"}, {"id": "h1", "kind": "topic", "tab": "more", "title": "H1 · Multiplexers (lecturer hinted)", "freq": "Not on 23/24 paper", "learn": "<p>A <b>multiplexer</b> is a switch: the <b>select</b> lines pick which <b>data</b> input reaches the output.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/mux4-block.svg\" alt=\"4-to-1 mux\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<p><b>Any function of n + 1 variables fits on a mux with n selects:</b></p>\n<ol><li>First n variables → select lines.</li><li>For each select value, look at F when the last variable is 0 and when it is 1.</li><li>Connect that data input to <b>0, 1, the variable, or its inverse</b>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt small\"><thead><tr><th>F for last variable = 0, 1</th><th>Connect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0, 0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0, 1</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>1, 0</td><td>D'</td></tr><tr><td>1, 1</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "check": {"q": "How many variables can one 8-to-1 mux implement?", "a": "<b>4.</b> Three on the selects, the fourth on the data inputs."}, "items": [{"id": "h1-1", "title": "H1.1 8-to-1 mux", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F(A, B, C, D) = \\Sigma m(0, 1, 3, 4, 8, 9, 15)$ with one 8-to-1 multiplexer.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "4 variables but only 3 select lines. Put A, B, C on the selects. The last variable, D, goes to the data inputs.", "why": "A mux with n selects can do a function of n + 1 variables, because each data input can be 0, 1, the last variable or its inverse."}, {"do": "Each select value covers two rows of the truth table: D = 0 and D = 1. Compare F on that pair:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>Input</th><th>D = 0</th><th>D = 1</th><th>Connect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>000</td><td>I<sub>0</sub></td><td>m<sub>0</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>1</sub> = 1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>001</td><td>I<sub>1</sub></td><td>m<sub>2</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>3</sub> = 1</td><td><b>D</b></td></tr><tr><td>010</td><td>I<sub>2</sub></td><td>m<sub>4</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>5</sub> = 0</td><td><b>D'</b></td></tr><tr><td>011</td><td>I<sub>3</sub></td><td>m<sub>6</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>7</sub> = 0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>I<sub>4</sub></td><td>m<sub>8</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>9</sub> = 1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>101</td><td>I<sub>5</sub></td><td>m<sub>10</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>11</sub> = 0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>110</td><td>I<sub>6</sub></td><td>m<sub>12</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>13</sub> = 0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>111</td><td>I<sub>7</sub></td><td>m<sub>14</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>15</sub> = 1</td><td><b>D</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Pair 0,0 → F is 0 whatever D is. Pair 1,1 → 1. Pair 0,1 → F copies D. Pair 1,0 → F is the inverse."}, {"do": "Data inputs I₀ … : 1, D, D', 0, 1, 0, 0, D", "why": "One NOT gate makes the inverted variable if it is needed."}], "src": "Lecture 10 · lecturer hinted", "answer": "1, D, D', 0, 1, 0, 0, D"}, {"id": "h1-2", "title": "H1.2 4-to-1 mux", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F(X, Y, Z) = \\Sigma m(1, 2, 4, 7)$ with one 4-to-1 multiplexer.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "3 variables but only 2 select lines. Put X, Y on the selects. The last variable, Z, goes to the data inputs.", "why": "A mux with n selects can do a function of n + 1 variables, because each data input can be 0, 1, the last variable or its inverse."}, {"do": "Each select value covers two rows of the truth table: Z = 0 and Z = 1. Compare F on that pair:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>XY</th><th>Input</th><th>Z = 0</th><th>Z = 1</th><th>Connect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>00</td><td>I<sub>0</sub></td><td>m<sub>0</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>1</sub> = 1</td><td><b>Z</b></td></tr><tr><td>01</td><td>I<sub>1</sub></td><td>m<sub>2</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>3</sub> = 0</td><td><b>Z'</b></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>I<sub>2</sub></td><td>m<sub>4</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>5</sub> = 0</td><td><b>Z'</b></td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>I<sub>3</sub></td><td>m<sub>6</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>7</sub> = 1</td><td><b>Z</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Pair 0,0 → F is 0 whatever Z is. Pair 1,1 → 1. Pair 0,1 → F copies Z. Pair 1,0 → F is the inverse."}, {"do": "Data inputs I₀ … : Z, Z', Z', Z", "why": "One NOT gate makes the inverted variable if it is needed."}], "src": "Lecture 10 · lecturer hinted", "answer": "Z, Z', Z', Z"}, {"id": "h1-3", "title": "H1.3 8-to-1 mux", "prompt": "<p>Implement $F(A, B, C, D) = \\Sigma m(2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 11, 12, 13, 14)$ with one 8-to-1 multiplexer.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "4 variables but only 3 select lines. Put A, B, C on the selects. The last variable, D, goes to the data inputs.", "why": "A mux with n selects can do a function of n + 1 variables, because each data input can be 0, 1, the last variable or its inverse."}, {"do": "Each select value covers two rows of the truth table: D = 0 and D = 1. Compare F on that pair:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>ABC</th><th>Input</th><th>D = 0</th><th>D = 1</th><th>Connect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>000</td><td>I<sub>0</sub></td><td>m<sub>0</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>1</sub> = 0</td><td><b>0</b></td></tr><tr><td>001</td><td>I<sub>1</sub></td><td>m<sub>2</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>3</sub> = 1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>010</td><td>I<sub>2</sub></td><td>m<sub>4</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>5</sub> = 1</td><td><b>D</b></td></tr><tr><td>011</td><td>I<sub>3</sub></td><td>m<sub>6</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>7</sub> = 0</td><td><b>D'</b></td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>I<sub>4</sub></td><td>m<sub>8</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>9</sub> = 0</td><td><b>D'</b></td></tr><tr><td>101</td><td>I<sub>5</sub></td><td>m<sub>10</sub> = 0</td><td>m<sub>11</sub> = 1</td><td><b>D</b></td></tr><tr><td>110</td><td>I<sub>6</sub></td><td>m<sub>12</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>13</sub> = 1</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>111</td><td>I<sub>7</sub></td><td>m<sub>14</sub> = 1</td><td>m<sub>15</sub> = 0</td><td><b>D'</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Pair 0,0 → F is 0 whatever D is. Pair 1,1 → 1. Pair 0,1 → F copies D. Pair 1,0 → F is the inverse."}, {"do": "Data inputs I₀ … : 0, 1, D, D', D', D, 1, D'", "why": "One NOT gate makes the inverted variable if it is needed."}], "src": "Lecture 10 · lecturer hinted", "answer": "0, 1, D, D', D', D, 1, D'"}]}, {"id": "h2", "kind": "topic", "tab": "more", "title": "H2 · Sequence detectors (likely swap for Q3)", "freq": "Not on 23/24 paper", "learn": "<p>Same design steps as Topic 7, but now there is an <b>input X</b>, so every state has <b>two</b> arrows out (X = 0 and X = 1), and an output Z.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><img src=\"../svg/seq/fsm-1001-mealy.svg\" alt=\"1001 detector\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Output depends on</th><th>Drawn</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Mealy</b></td><td>state and input</td><td>on the arrow: input / output</td></tr><tr><td><b>Moore</b></td><td>state only</td><td>in the circle. Needs one more state.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule for the diagram:</b> each state = how much of the pattern you have. On any bit, go to the state for the <b>longest ending that is still a start of the pattern</b>.</p>\n<p>Then: state table → codes → excitation table → K-maps (variables Q₁, Q₀, X) → circuit.</p>", "check": {"q": "Detecting 110. You have 11 and another 1 arrives. Which state next?", "a": "<b>Stay in \"have 11\".</b> The input ends in 111, and its last two bits are still 11."}, "items": [{"id": "h2-1", "title": "H2.1 Detect 101 (JK)", "prompt": "<p>Design a Mealy circuit with input X and output Z. Z = 1 when the last 3 inputs were <b>101</b> (overlap allowed). Use JK flip-flops.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "States = how much of 101 you have so far:<ul><li>A = nothing</li><li>B = have 1</li><li>C = have 10</li></ul>", "why": "A detector only needs to remember the useful tail of the input. 3 states → 2 flip-flops."}, {"do": "Happy path first: A —1→ → B —0→ C, and from C the bit 1 completes the pattern (Z = 1).", "why": "Draw the arrows that follow the pattern before worrying about anything else."}, {"do": "Every other arrow: go to the state for the <b>longest ending of the input that is still a start of the pattern</b>. State table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>X</th><th>Next</th><th>Z</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>A 00</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>C 10</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "After a wrong bit you rarely go all the way back. After a match, the last bits may already start the next match (overlap)."}, {"do": "Add the J K columns from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>X</th><th>Next</th><th>Z</th><th>$J_{1}$ $K_{1}$</th><th>$J_{0}$ $K_{0}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>A 00</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>x 1</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0 → J = next bit, K = x. Present bit 1 → K = opposite of next bit, J = x."}, {"do": "K-map each column (variables Q₁, Q₀, X; code 11 is unused → don't care):$$J_{1} = Q_{0}X'$$$$J_{0} = X$$$$K_{1} = 1$$$$K_{0} = X'$$$$Z = Q_{1}X$$", "why": "Unused state codes can never occur, so they are x in every map."}], "src": "Lectures 14–16 · not on 23/24 paper", "answer": "$J_{1} = Q_{0}X'$ · $J_{0} = X$ · $K_{1} = 1$ · $K_{0} = X'$ · $Z = Q_{1}X$"}, {"id": "h2-2", "title": "H2.2 Detect 110 (D)", "prompt": "<p>Design a Mealy circuit with input X and output Z. Z = 1 when the last 3 inputs were <b>110</b> (overlap allowed). Use D flip-flops.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "States = how much of 110 you have so far:<ul><li>A = nothing</li><li>B = have 1</li><li>C = have 11</li></ul>", "why": "A detector only needs to remember the useful tail of the input. 3 states → 2 flip-flops."}, {"do": "Happy path first: A —1→ → B —1→ C, and from C the bit 0 completes the pattern (Z = 1).", "why": "Draw the arrows that follow the pattern before worrying about anything else."}, {"do": "Every other arrow: go to the state for the <b>longest ending of the input that is still a start of the pattern</b>. State table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>X</th><th>Next</th><th>Z</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>A 00</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>C 10</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>1</td><td>C 10</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "After a wrong bit you rarely go all the way back. After a match, the last bits may already start the next match (overlap)."}, {"do": "With D flip-flops, D = next state. The Next column <b>is</b> D₁D₀.", "why": "Q⁺ = D, so there is no excitation step."}, {"do": "K-map each column (variables Q₁, Q₀, X; code 11 is unused → don't care):$$D_{1} = Q_{0}X + Q_{1}X$$$$D_{0} = Q_{1}'Q_{0}'X$$$$Z = Q_{1}X'$$", "why": "Unused state codes can never occur, so they are x in every map."}], "src": "Lectures 14–16 · not on 23/24 paper", "answer": "$D_{1} = Q_{0}X + Q_{1}X$ · $D_{0} = Q_{1}'Q_{0}'X$ · $Z = Q_{1}X'$"}, {"id": "h2-3", "title": "H2.3 Detect 1001 (JK)", "prompt": "<p>Design a Mealy circuit with input X and output Z. Z = 1 when the last 4 inputs were <b>1001</b> (overlap allowed). Use JK flip-flops.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "States = how much of 1001 you have so far:<ul><li>A = nothing</li><li>B = have 1</li><li>C = have 10</li><li>D = have 100</li></ul>", "why": "A detector only needs to remember the useful tail of the input. 4 states → 2 flip-flops."}, {"do": "Happy path first: A —1→ → B —0→ → C —0→ D, and from D the bit 1 completes the pattern (Z = 1).", "why": "Draw the arrows that follow the pattern before worrying about anything else."}, {"do": "Every other arrow: go to the state for the <b>longest ending of the input that is still a start of the pattern</b>. State table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>X</th><th>Next</th><th>Z</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>A 00</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>C 10</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>D 11</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>D 11</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>D 11</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "After a wrong bit you rarely go all the way back. After a match, the last bits may already start the next match (overlap)."}, {"do": "Add the J K columns from the excitation table:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>X</th><th>Next</th><th>Z</th><th>$J_{1}$ $K_{1}$</th><th>$J_{0}$ $K_{0}$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>0 x</td><td>0 x</td></tr><tr><td>A 00</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>0 x</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>1 x</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>0 x</td><td>x 0</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>0</td><td>D 11</td><td>0</td><td>x 0</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>C 10</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>0</td><td>x 1</td><td>1 x</td></tr><tr><td>D 11</td><td>0</td><td>A 00</td><td>0</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 1</td></tr><tr><td>D 11</td><td>1</td><td>B 01</td><td>1</td><td>x 1</td><td>x 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Present bit 0 → J = next bit, K = x. Present bit 1 → K = opposite of next bit, J = x."}, {"do": "K-map each column (variables Q₁, Q₀, X):$$J_{1} = Q_{0}X'$$$$J_{0} = X + Q_{1}$$$$K_{1} = X + Q_{0}$$$$K_{0} = X'$$$$Z = Q_{1}Q_{0}X$$", "why": "Unused state codes can never occur, so they are x in every map."}], "src": "Lectures 14–16 · not on 23/24 paper", "answer": "$J_{1} = Q_{0}X'$ · $J_{0} = X + Q_{1}$ · $K_{1} = X + Q_{0}$ · $K_{0} = X'$ · $Z = Q_{1}Q_{0}X$"}]}, {"id": "h3", "kind": "topic", "tab": "more", "title": "H3 · Analysing a clocked circuit", "freq": "Not on 23/24 paper", "learn": "<p><b>Analysis</b> = design backwards: circuit → equations → state table → state diagram.</p>\n<ol><li>Read the equation for each flip-flop input (and the output).</li><li>List every present state and input. Work out the flip-flop inputs on each row.</li><li>Use the characteristic table to get the next state (JK: 00 hold, 01 reset, 10 set, 11 toggle).</li></ol>\n<p>Check: n flip-flops → 2ⁿ circles, and with one input, 2 arrows leave every circle.</p>", "check": {"q": "Present Q = 1, J K = 1 1. Next Q?", "a": "<b>0.</b> 11 toggles."}, "items": [{"id": "h3-1", "title": "H3.1 JK circuit", "prompt": "<p>Two JK flip-flops A and B, input x: $J_{A} = B$, $K_{A} = Bx'$, $J_{B} = x'$, $K_{B} = A \\oplus x$. Derive the state table.</p>", "steps": [{"do": "8 rows: every present state A B with x = 0 and 1.", "why": "2 flip-flops + 1 input = 2³ combinations."}, {"do": "Work out J and K for each flip-flop from the <b>present</b> state, then apply the JK action:<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"tt\"><thead><tr><th>A B</th><th>x</th><th>$J_{A}$ $K_{A}$</th><th>$J_{B}$ $K_{B}$</th><th>A⁺ B⁺</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 0</td><td>0</td><td>0 0</td><td>1 0</td><td><b>0 1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0 0</td><td>1</td><td>0 0</td><td>0 1</td><td><b>0 0</b></td></tr><tr><td>0 1</td><td>0</td><td>1 1</td><td>1 0</td><td><b>1 1</b></td></tr><tr><td>0 1</td><td>1</td><td>1 0</td><td>0 1</td><td><b>1 0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1 0</td><td>0</td><td>0 0</td><td>1 1</td><td><b>1 1</b></td></tr><tr><td>1 0</td><td>1</td><td>0 0</td><td>0 0</td><td><b>1 0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1 1</td><td>0</td><td>1 1</td><td>1 1</td><td><b>0 0</b></td></tr><tr><td>1 1</td><td>1</td><td>1 0</td><td>0 0</td><td><b>1 1</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "why": "Example row 0 1, x = 0: J K for A = 1 1 → toggle → 1. J K for B = 1 0 → set → 1. Next state 1 1."}, {"do": "State diagram: 4 circles, arrows labelled with x. 00 —0→ 01, 00 —1→ 00, 01 —0→ 11, 01 —1→ 10, 10 —0→ 11, 10 —1→ 10, 11 —0→ 00, 11 —1→ 11.", "why": "One arrow per table row."}], "src": "Lecture 13 style", "answer": "see table"}]}]};
